  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unseasonable warmth continues for the next couple of \ndays.\n\nHigh pressure centered to our SE will maintain southwest winds \nthrough tonight, becoming more southerly on Thursday. As of 1 PM this \nafternoon, no record highs have been tied or broken but \ntemperatures are within a few degrees of the records. See the \nclimate section below for more details.\n\nThere may be record maximum low temperatures for Thursday and \nFriday, and high temperatures for Thursday at least approach records \nfor the day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Wet and unsettled weather returns Thursday into this \nweekend. Continued above normal temperatures before cooler \nconditions arrive Sunday and beyond.\n\nChances of rain begin on Thursday, mainly over the western \nzones, but no impacts expected during the day. A cold front \nslowing its eastward progression then enters the region late \nThursday night. A wave of low pressure is progged to form along \nthis front as a closed 500mb low approaches the Northeast. This \ncombination will help to bring a prolonged period of rainfall \nwith the highest overall chances of rain occurring Friday \nmorning through early Saturday morning. \n\nRainfall could be heavy at times Friday and Friday night. PWATs will \nlikely be in the range of 1.50 to 1.75 inches and perhaps enough \nCAPE over SE zones for a rumble of thunder and localized heavier \ndownpours. Extra synoptic lift is anticipated during this period as \nwell with the region progged to be in the right entrance region of \nan upper jet streak. The higher rainfall amounts are anticipated \nover the SE zones. Fortunately, we've been very dry and flash flood \nguidance is high. Still not expecting flash flooding to be an issue, \nthough potential rain amounts have increased from the previous \nforecast and will need to see if guidance trends wetter and is more \nconsistent with the location of the axis of highest precip. Some \npoor drainage/urban flooding is possible, especially given the \nleaves blocking drainage this time of year. There continues to be a \nmarginal risk within the Excessive Rainfall Outlook for Friday into \nSaturday.\n\nRain chances drop off Saturday morning but showers will continue to \nbe possible through Sunday morning with a cyclonic flow overhead; \nnothing like Friday/Friday night though. NW flow ushers in a colder, \nalbeit closer to normal, airmass for early next week with high \ntemperatures returning to the 50s.",
